      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "We conducted a cross sectional survey of electrocardiographic changes among shift-workers."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We classified the electrocardiogram morphology, and measured the QTc interval in 91 male workers engaged in regular daily work-shifts, 32 in 24 hr work-shift (h24), and 93 in irregular 6 hr work-shift (h6)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "With reference to daily workers, the QTc interval was prolonged among h6 workers (P < 0.001) and h24 workers (P < 0.005). The age- and obesity-adjusted standardized prevalence ratio (SPR) of a borderline/prolonged QTc was 2.2-fold among h6 workers (95% CI 1.2, 4.2); conduction disorders (SPR = 2.6; 95% CI 1.3, 5.2) and repolarization disorders (SPR = 1.9; 95% CI 1.0, 3.5) were also more frequently observed among h6 workers. Excluding 19 subjects with risk factors for prolonged QTc did not change the results."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Our study reveals significant changes in the ECG morphology in relation to shift-work, especially in unpredictable and non-standard working hours."
          }
        ]
      },
